      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_706d285ba52758b810b81063",
        "timestamp": 1778876889000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "walkableroanoke",
        "username": "Walkable Roanoke",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@reedinthewind Thanks for the engagement! Actually I agree significantly - targeted zoning, i.e., targeting the city (rather than aiming for the county) allows cities to be more dense and walkable. What single-family zoning means is not just restriction on builders, but on property owners who cannot rent their ADUs or divide their home into a duplex. With single-family-only, the current incentive for builders to only build at the extremes - the McMansions or the big apartment buildings. This zoning reform allows the missing middle housing options to exist in the city.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 3,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T20:28:09Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_a7d7407f145b1154b864652d",
        "timestamp": 1778876971000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "walkableroanoke",
        "username": "Walkable Roanoke",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@reedinthewind Also good point about vacant lots/homes - that's a piece of addressing the housing crisis, too, especially via tax reforms.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 4,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T20:29:31Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_38fe7e7534949e489b00e957",
        "timestamp": 1778877182000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "walkableroanoke",
        "username": "Walkable Roanoke",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@arielclark Certainly agree that none of the solutions we're advocating for rely on the ethical bearings of developers. The StrongTowns approach is aimed at incremental, bottom-up solutions made possible by giving greater freedom to residents. That freedom does not extend to controlling their neighbors, but allows them live in arrangements other than single-family.\nAnd I do agree, I love our neighborhoods! Demand is definitely one way to frame it - supply of missing middle options is a big piece of it, too - https://www.strongtowns.org/journal/2026-2-26-three-ways-of-understanding-the-housing-crisis",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 5,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T20:33:02Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_bd95a01900f98eeb8f1b8c88",
        "timestamp": 1778877542000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "walkableroanoke",
        "username": "Walkable Roanoke",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@reedinthewind Re: infrastructure, certainly a valid concern. The main challenge with infrastructure, however, is not the strain of high-density development, but rather the long-term financial insolvency caused by low-density, sprawling development patterns. Utilizing and maintaining existing infrastructure is a more cost-effective model than the suburban sprawl model. Again, the point of zoning reform is to allow the city to be the city; the suburbs can keep doing their single-family only, spread-out, car-centered thing",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 6,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T20:39:02Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "18115570264808850",
        "timestamp": 1778806683000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "walkableroanoke",
        "username": "Walkable Roanoke",
        "parent_comment_id": null,
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "We appreciate the shares and signups!\nIn fairness, I suspect most people who want single-family-zoning-only are worried not about walkability, but rather they are worried about rapid densification (all-the-sudden high rise apts). These reforms actually help us AVOID rapid densification, because it allows for incremental development that increases with demand.\nIn fairness also in the opposite direction, one of the main backers of the lawsuit against the city (to fight for exclusionary zoning) has publicly criticized those wanting zoning reform by saying derogatory comments about us acting like Roanoke was supposed to be a place like Chicago “where you walk around.” So… those most in the know on all sides I believe understand that walkability requires density, which requires getting rid of restrictive zoning codes that ban all housing except single-family-lots.\nThe fear is the loss of investment, home values, and neighborhood change. The ironic piece is that walkable, human-scale density (villages, not Manhattans), is so desirable that those places make the land grow in value a ton.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18115570264808850",
        "source_message_index": 1,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18115570264808850",
        "source_native_comment_id": "18115570264808850",
        "source_message_role": "root_comment",
        "parent_link_basis": "root_comment",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T00:58:03Z"
      },
