  "reedinthewind": {
    "user_id": "reedinthewind",
    "username": "Andrew Downs",
    "comment_count": 4,
    "comments": [
      {
        "comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "timestamp": 1778845252000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "reedinthewind",
        "username": "Andrew Downs",
        "parent_comment_id": null,
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "I’m for keeping single family zoning. I agree there is a problem, but I don’t think eliminating single family zoning is the solution. Targeted, not blanket rezoning and a focus on derelict homes and structures. Developers won’t build the homes we need, they’ll build to make money and often that means cheaply made but larger sqf homes jammed into small lots.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 1,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "source_message_role": "root_comment",
        "parent_link_basis": "root_comment",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T11:40:52Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_24b67ad12bbbb83928c562aa",
        "timestamp": 1778859969000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "reedinthewind",
        "username": "Andrew Downs",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@arielclark exactly. I think there is also a downward spiral effect. Affordable housing can be addressed by making cheaper housing AND by attracting higher-paying employers. What do higher-paying employers want? Single family neighborhoods that haven’t been retrofitted to accommodate twice the strain on infrastructure. We’re training the public to settle for less instead of demanding more. Beautiful NW neighborhoods are full of vacants. Focus on maximizing our existing housing stock and you’ll attract employers that raise the average pay in the City.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 2,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T15:46:09Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_3b4c917136bd35662a4f4995",
        "timestamp": 1778898718000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "reedinthewind",
        "username": "Andrew Downs",
        "parent_comment_id": "18074211125281427",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@walkableroanoke yea, I think you need a tighter strategic focus than city/county. More streets in my neighborhood could be rezoned for the missing middle, but others will not work. Also the whole argument: “young people want to buy single family homes but can’t afford them so let’s just eliminate all single family homes” doesn’t seem right. Rezone about 60%? Sure, but have a plan. Restore downtown buildings to build housing, develop vacant land, and see if we still have a problem. Until then, leave the housing stock that people clearly want alone.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18074211125281427",
        "source_message_index": 7,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18074211125281427",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-16T02:31:58Z"
      },
      {
        "comment_id": "igreply_b296413038ed89144cbdace8",
        "timestamp": 1778875208000,
        "ip_location": "",
        "post_id": "DYVNstAGTri",
        "user_id": "reedinthewind",
        "username": "Andrew Downs",
        "parent_comment_id": "18115570264808850",
        "at_count": 0,
        "content": "@walkableroanoke just a note. I am against this blanket approach to rezoning and for a walkable/bike-able Roanoke. My main concern is that this strategy relies on developers to build what we want rather than what will make them the most money. I’ve also seen this type of retrofit damage bikeability. Build new denser neighborhoods where we can start from the ground up (Evans spring), take a measured and strategic approach to rezoning based on clear criteria and develop incentives for derelict housing improvement (which is a money making investment). Respectfully.",
        "sub_comment_count": 0,
        "source_thread_id": "id:18115570264808850",
        "source_message_index": 2,
        "source_root_comment_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DYVNstAGTri?comment_id=18115570264808850",
        "source_native_comment_id": null,
        "source_message_role": "reply",
        "parent_link_basis": "source_thread_membership_not_inferred_direct_reply",
        "time_iso_utc": "2026-05-15T20:00:08Z"
      }
    ]
  },
